import Prelude
/-!
# Logic

Propositional logic over the impredicative `Prop` (stage 24a).

Wiz has no inductive propositions (`μ` always lives in `Type`), so the connectives are
built from what the kernel has: `True`, `False`, `absurd`, Σ and Π, and observational
equality (CoreSpec §7–8).

* `∧` is a Σ of two propositions, so it is itself a proposition, with projections and η.
* `↔` is the pair `(P → Q) × (Q → P)`. An equation of propositions computes to almost
  the same type (propext, CoreSpec §8.2), `(0 _ : (0 _ : P) → Q) × ((0 _ : Q) → P)`,
  with erased binders, so `Iff.toEq` and `Iff.ofEq` are one-liners (and no axiom).
  The two differ in the Σ binder's quantity: the first field of a computed equation
  can't be projected outside erased code, so `Iff.ofEq` gets it from `Eq.symm`.
* `∨` and `∃` are Church encodings (the impredicative ones: `∀ R, (… → R) → R`). A Σ
  over a `Type` would land in `Type`, and there are no inductive propositions to define
  them with. Their eliminators only go into propositions, which is all a logic needs
  (and is what Lean's `Or.elim`/`Exists.elim` allow too). The eliminated proposition
  `R` is bound at quantity 0.

The names are Lean's, so that the library can switch to native inductive propositions
later (plan D4) without renaming.

What is here: `Not` (`¬`), `And` (`∧`), `Or` (`∨`), `Iff` (`↔`), `Ex` (`∃`, with binder
notation), the equality lemmas `Eq.symm`, `Eq.trans`, `Eq.congrArg`, `Eq.substProp`,
the definitional `propext` and `funext`, and the Boolean algebra (`Bool.and_comm`,
`Bool.or_assoc`, `Bool.and_absorb`, the De Morgan laws, …) with its bridges from
`b = true` to `∧`, `∨` and `¬` (`Bool.and_eq_true`, `Bool.or_eq_true`, `Bool.not_eq_true`).
The oriented Boolean identities are registered with `attribute [simp]` at the end of the
file, so `simp` normalises Boolean goals without a rule list.

```
import Logic

def swap {P Q : Prop} (h : P ∧ Q) : Q ∧ P := And.intro h.2 h.1
def orSwap {P Q : Prop} (h : P ∨ Q) : Q ∨ P := Or.elim h Or.inr Or.inl
def ex3 : ∃ n : Nat, n = 3 := Ex.intro (fun n => n = 3) 3 rfl
```
-/

-- ## Truth, falsity, negation

/-- The proof of `True` (it is the unit type). -/
def True.intro : True := ()
/-- Ex falso: from a proof of `False`, any proposition. -/
def False.elim {0 P : Prop} (h : False) : P := absurd h
/-- `¬ P`, negation: a proof of `P` would give `False`. -/
def Not (P : Prop) : Prop := P → False
notation:max "¬" p:40 => Not p
/-- From `¬ P` and `P`, anything. -/
def Not.elim {P : Prop} {0 R : Prop} (n : Not P) (p : P) : R := absurd (n p)
/-- `Not.elim` with the arguments the other way round: from `P` and `¬ P`, anything. -/
def absurdOf {P : Prop} {0 R : Prop} (p : P) (n : Not P) : R := absurd (n p)

-- ## Conjunction

/-- `P ∧ Q` (also `P /\ Q`): a pair of proofs. A Σ of two propositions, so a proposition
itself, with projections `.1`/`.2` and η. -/
def And (P Q : Prop) : Prop := P × Q
-- (the ASCII spellings first: the notation declared last is the one printed, 11r)
infixr:35 " /\\ " => And
infixr:35 " ∧ " => And
/-- Prove `P ∧ Q` from both halves. -/
def And.intro {P Q : Prop} (p : P) (q : Q) : P ∧ Q := (p, q)
/-- The left half of a conjunction. -/
def And.left {P Q : Prop} (h : P ∧ Q) : P := h.1
/-- The right half of a conjunction. -/
def And.right {P Q : Prop} (h : P ∧ Q) : Q := h.2
/-- Use both halves of a conjunction at once: `And.elim h (fun p q => …)`. -/
def And.elim {P Q : Prop} {0 R : Prop} (h : P ∧ Q) (k : P → Q → R) : R := k h.1 h.2
/-- `∧` is commutative. -/
def And.symm {P Q : Prop} (h : P ∧ Q) : Q ∧ P := (h.2, h.1)
/-- Map both halves of a conjunction. -/
def And.imp {P Q P' Q' : Prop} (f : P → P') (g : Q → Q') (h : P ∧ Q) : P' ∧ Q' := (f h.1, g h.2)

-- ## Disjunction

/-- `P ∨ Q` (also `P \/ Q`), Church-encoded: for every proposition `R`, a way to get `R`
from either side gives `R`. Build it with `Or.inl`/`Or.inr`, use it with `Or.elim`. -/
def Or (P Q : Prop) : Prop := (0 R : Prop) → (P → R) → (Q → R) → R
infixr:30 " \\/ " => Or
infixr:30 " ∨ " => Or
/-- The left injection: `P` proves `P ∨ Q`. -/
def Or.inl {P Q : Prop} (p : P) : P ∨ Q := fun R l r => l p
/-- The right injection: `Q` proves `P ∨ Q`. -/
def Or.inr {P Q : Prop} (q : Q) : P ∨ Q := fun R l r => r q
/-- Case analysis on a disjunction, into a proposition `R`.

```
Or.elim h (fun p => …) (fun q => …)
``` -/
def Or.elim {P Q : Prop} {0 R : Prop} (h : P ∨ Q) (l : P → R) (r : Q → R) : R := h R l r
/-- `∨` is commutative. -/
def Or.symm {P Q : Prop} (h : P ∨ Q) : Q ∨ P := h (Q ∨ P) (fun p => Or.inr p) (fun q => Or.inl q)
/-- Map both sides of a disjunction. -/
def Or.imp {P Q P' Q' : Prop} (f : P → P') (g : Q → Q') (h : P ∨ Q) : P' ∨ Q' :=
  h (P' ∨ Q') (fun p => Or.inl (f p)) (fun q => Or.inr (g q))
/-- The disjunctive syllogism: from `P ∨ Q` and `¬ P`, `Q`. -/
def Or.resolveLeft {P Q : Prop} (h : P ∨ Q) (n : ¬ P) : Q := h Q (fun p => absurd (n p)) (fun q => q)
/-- From `P ∨ Q` and `¬ Q`, `P`. -/
def Or.resolveRight {P Q : Prop} (h : P ∨ Q) (n : ¬ Q) : P := h P (fun p => p) (fun q => absurd (n q))

-- ## Equivalence

/-- `P ↔ Q` (also `P <-> Q`): implications both ways. By `propext` it is the same as
`P = Q`. -/
def Iff (P Q : Prop) : Prop := (P → Q) × (Q → P)
infix:20 " <-> " => Iff
infix:20 " ↔ " => Iff
/-- Prove `P ↔ Q` from both implications. -/
def Iff.intro {P Q : Prop} (f : P → Q) (g : Q → P) : P ↔ Q := (f, g)
/-- The forward implication of an equivalence ("modus ponens"). -/
def Iff.mp {P Q : Prop} (h : P ↔ Q) (p : P) : Q := h.1 p
/-- The backward implication of an equivalence. -/
def Iff.mpr {P Q : Prop} (h : P ↔ Q) (q : Q) : P := h.2 q
/-- Every proposition is equivalent to itself. -/
def Iff.refl (P : Prop) : P ↔ P := (fun p => p, fun p => p)
/-- `↔` is symmetric. -/
def Iff.symm {P Q : Prop} (h : P ↔ Q) : Q ↔ P := (h.2, h.1)
/-- `↔` is transitive. -/
def Iff.trans {P Q R : Prop} (h₁ : P ↔ Q) (h₂ : Q ↔ R) : P ↔ R :=
  (fun p => h₂.1 (h₁.1 p), fun r => h₁.2 (h₂.2 r))

-- ## Existential quantification

/-- `∃ a, P a`, Church-encoded: for every proposition `R`, a way to get `R` from any
witness and its proof gives `R`. Write it with the binder notation `∃ x, P x` (or
`exists x, P x`); build it with `Ex.intro`, use it with `Ex.elim`. -/
def Ex {A : Type} (P : A → Prop) : Prop := (0 R : Prop) → ((a : A) → P a → R) → R
/-- Prove `∃ a, P a` from a witness and its proof: `Ex.intro (fun n => n = 3) 3 rfl`.
The predicate is explicit (it can't be inferred from the witness). -/
def Ex.intro {A : Type} (0 P : A → Prop) (a : A) (p : P a) : Ex P := fun R k => k a p
/-- Use an existential: get `R` from any witness and its proof. -/
def Ex.elim {A : Type} {P : A → Prop} {0 R : Prop} (h : Ex P) (k : (a : A) → P a → R) : R := h R k
/-- Map the proof inside an existential, keeping the witness. -/
def Ex.imp {A : Type} {P Q : A → Prop} (f : (a : A) → P a → Q a) (h : Ex P) : Ex Q :=
  h (Ex Q) (fun a p => Ex.intro Q a (f a p))

-- ### Binder notation (stage 13e)
--
-- `∃ x, P x` is `Ex (fun x => P x)`; binders as in `fun`, with an optional type for all
-- of them: `∃ x y, P`, `∃ x y : A, P`, `∃ (x : A) (y : B), P`, mixed `∃ x (y : B), P`.
-- `exists` is the ASCII spelling (as `forall` is for `∀`). The body extends as far as
-- possible, like `∀`'s. A procedural macro builds the nest of `Ex`s (quotations can't
-- splice lists).
declare_syntax_cat exBinder
syntax (name := plain) ident : exBinder
syntax (name := typed) "(" ident+ ":" term ")" : exBinder
syntax:lead (name := ex) "∃ " exBinder+ (":" term)? "," term : term
syntax:lead (name := exAscii) "exists" exBinder+ (":" term)? "," term : term
/-- `Ex (fun x => body)`, with the binder's type if there is one (for the `∃` macro). -/
def Ex.wrap (x : Syntax) (ty : Option Syntax) (body : Syntax) : Syntax := match ty with
  | .none => `(term| Ex (fun $x => $body))
  | .some A => `(term| Ex (fun ($x : $A) => $body))
/-- One `Ex` per identifier of a binder group, all with the type `ty` (for the `∃` macro). -/
def Ex.wrapIdents {b : Bool} (ty : Option Syntax) (body : Syntax) : SyntaxF b → Syntax
  | .cons x rest => Ex.wrap x ty (Ex.wrapIdents ty body rest)
  | _ => body
/-- The nest of `Ex`s for a list of binders: a plain binder takes the type after the
binders (if any), a typed one its own (for the `∃` macro). -/
def Ex.wrapBinders {b : Bool} (ty : Option Syntax) (body : Syntax) : SyntaxF b → Syntax
  | .cons bnd rest =>
    let inner := Ex.wrapBinders ty body rest;
    match Syntax.getArg bnd 1 with
    | .node _ xs => Ex.wrapIdents (.some (Syntax.getArg bnd 3)) inner xs
    | _ => Ex.wrap (Syntax.getArg bnd 0) ty inner
  | _ => body
/-- The optional `: A` after the binders of an `∃`. -/
def Ex.binderType (opt : Syntax) : Option Syntax := match Syntax.getArg opt 1 with
  | .missing => .none
  | A => .some A
/-- The procedural macro behind `∃ x y : A, P` and `exists x y : A, P`: it builds the
nest of `Ex`s (quotations can't splice lists). -/
def Ex.binderMacro : Macro := fun stx => match Syntax.getArg stx 1 with
  | .node _ bs => MacroM.pure (Ex.wrapBinders (Ex.binderType (Syntax.getArg stx 2)) (Syntax.getArg stx 4) bs)
  | _ => MacroM.throw "∃: malformed binders"
attribute [macro term.ex] Ex.binderMacro
attribute [macro term.exAscii] Ex.binderMacro
-- printed (11r): `Ex (fun x => P x)` is `∃ x, P x`
app_unexpander Ex
  | `(term| $f (fun $x => $b)) => `(term| ∃ $x, $b)
/-- `∃` commutes with `∧` on a side that doesn't mention the witness. -/
def Ex.andRight {A : Type} {P : A → Prop} {Q : Prop} (h : (∃ a, P a) ∧ Q) : ∃ a, P a ∧ Q :=
  h.1 (∃ a, P a ∧ Q) (fun a p => Ex.intro (fun a => P a ∧ Q) a (p, h.2))

-- ## Universal quantification
--
-- `∀` is the Π type `(a : A) → P a`, a proposition whenever `P a` is (impredicativity).
-- `∀` and `∃` interact as usual:
/-- If some `a` has `P a`, it is not the case that every `a` has `¬ P a`. -/
def Ex.notForall {A : Type} {P : A → Prop} (h : ∃ a, P a) (n : ∀ a, ¬ P a) : False :=
  h False (fun a p => n a p)
/-- If no `a` has `P a`, every `a` has `¬ P a`. -/
def Forall.notEx {A : Type} {P : A → Prop} (n : ¬ ∃ a, P a) (a : A) : ¬ P a :=
  fun p => n (Ex.intro P a p)

-- ## Equality
--
-- `transp` is the eliminator; these are J in its usual shapes. (lib/Optic.wiz has the
-- same lemmas under `Optic.*`.)
/-- Equality is symmetric (`transp` with the motive `fun b _ => b = a`). -/
def Eq.symm {A : Type} {a b : A} (e : a = b) : b = a :=
  transp (fun b _ => b = a) (refl a) e
/-- Equality is transitive. -/
def Eq.trans {A : Type} {a b c : A} (e₁ : a = b) (e₂ : b = c) : a = c :=
  transp (fun c _ => a = c) e₁ e₂
/-- Equal arguments give equal results: from `a = b`, `f a = f b`. -/
def Eq.congrArg {A B : Type} (0 f : A → B) {a b : A} (e : a = b) : f a = f b :=
  transp (fun b _ => f a = f b) (refl (f a)) e
/-- Transport a proof of `P a` along `a = b` to a proof of `P b`, for a family of
propositions `P`.

(It goes through `congrArg`: a motive `fun b _ => P b` would use `b`, which `transp`
binds at 0; an equation of propositions computes to the pair of implications, whose
second field is the direction we want.) -/
def Eq.substProp {A : Type} (0 P : A → Prop) {a b : A} (e : a = b) (pa : P a) : P b :=
  (@Eq.congrArg A Prop P b a (@Eq.symm A a b e)).2 pa
/-- Equivalent propositions are equal: propositional extensionality is definitional
(`P = Q` computes to a pair of implications). -/
def Iff.toEq {P Q : Prop} (h : P ↔ Q) : P = Q := (h.1, h.2)
/-- Equal propositions are equivalent. -/
def Iff.ofEq {P Q : Prop} (e : P = Q) : P ↔ Q := ((@Eq.symm Prop P Q e).2, e.2)
/-- Propositional extensionality, `(P ↔ Q) → P = Q`: a theorem (`Iff.toEq`), not an axiom. -/
def propext {P Q : Prop} (h : P ↔ Q) : P = Q := Iff.toEq h
/-- Function extensionality is definitional: an equation of functions *is* pointwise,
`(0 x : A) → f x = g x`. Its binder is erased, so a proof that uses `x` (passes it to a
lemma that matches on it) can't be written against it directly; `funext` takes the
unerased form and lets subtyping (quantities don't matter in a Π that is a
proposition, CoreSpec §11.5) do the rest. -/
def funext {A B : Type} {f g : A → B} (h : (x : A) → f x = g x) : f = g := h
/-- Equal functions agree at every argument: from `f = g`, `f x = g x`. -/
def congrFun {A B : Type} {f g : A → B} (e : f = g) (x : A) : f x = g x := e x

-- ## Composing relations: `Trans` and `calc`

/-- `Trans p q r`: a proof of `p` and one of `q` give one of `r`. `calc` (stage 13s)
composes adjacent steps with an instance of it: the relations may differ (`a ≤ b` then
`b < c` gives `a < c`), and the last step's relation is the chain's.

`r` is an `outParam`, so instance search finds it from `p` and `q`. A library registers
a relation by adding instances (`lib/Nat.wiz`, `lib/IntOrder.wiz`, `lib/Rat.wiz`), and
so can a program:

```
def MyRel (a b : Nat) : Prop := a = b
infix:50 " ~ " => MyRel
instance {a b c : Nat} : Trans (a ~ b) (b ~ c) (a ~ c) := Trans.mk (fun h h' => Eq.trans h h')
``` -/
class Trans (p q : Prop) (r : outParam Prop) where
  /-- Compose a proof of `p` and one of `q` into a proof of `r`. -/
  trans : p → q → r

/-- `=` composes with itself. -/
instance {A : Type} {a b c : A} : Trans (a = b) (b = c) (a = c) :=
  Trans.mk (fun e₁ e₂ => Eq.trans e₁ e₂)
/-- `=` between propositions is transitive. (The generic `Eq.trans` is at a `Type`, and
`Prop` is a `Sort`, not a `Type`.) -/
def Eq.transProp {P Q R : Prop} (e₁ : P = Q) (e₂ : Q = R) : P = R :=
  Iff.toEq (Iff.trans (Iff.ofEq e₁) (Iff.ofEq e₂))
/-- `=` between propositions composes with itself. (The generic instance does not apply:
equations of propositions live at `Prop`, not at a `Type`.) -/
instance {P Q R : Prop} : Trans (P = Q) (Q = R) (P = R) :=
  Trans.mk (fun e₁ e₂ => Eq.transProp e₁ e₂)
/-- `↔` composes with itself. -/
instance {P Q R : Prop} : Trans (P ↔ Q) (Q ↔ R) (P ↔ R) :=
  Trans.mk (fun h₁ h₂ => Iff.trans h₁ h₂)

-- ## Booleans

/-- Every boolean is `.true` or `.false`. -/
def Bool.cases (b : Bool) : b = .true ∨ b = .false := match b with
  | .true => Or.inl rfl
  | .false => Or.inr rfl
/-- `.true` and `.false` differ. (`.true = .false` computes to a Σ whose first component
is `False`; `absurd` checks its argument erased, so it may project that erased field.) -/
def Bool.trueNeFalse : ¬ Bool.true = Bool.false := fun e => absurd e.1
/-- The left conjunct of a true `Bool.and`. -/
def Bool.and_left : (a b : Bool) → Bool.and a b = .true → a = .true
  | .true, _, _ => rfl
  | .false, _, h => absurd h.1
/-- The right conjunct of a true `Bool.and`. -/
def Bool.and_right : (a b : Bool) → Bool.and a b = .true → b = .true
  | .true, _, h => h
  | .false, _, h => absurd h.1

-- ## Boolean algebra

/-- `Bool.and` (`a && b`) is commutative. -/
def Bool.and_comm : (a b : Bool) → Bool.and a b = Bool.and b a
  | .true, .true => rfl
  | .true, .false => rfl
  | .false, .true => rfl
  | .false, .false => rfl
/-- `Bool.and` is associative. -/
def Bool.and_assoc : (a b c : Bool) → Bool.and (Bool.and a b) c = Bool.and a (Bool.and b c)
  | .true, b, c => rfl
  | .false, b, c => rfl
/-- `Bool.or` (`a || b`) is commutative. -/
def Bool.or_comm : (a b : Bool) → Bool.or a b = Bool.or b a
  | .true, .true => rfl
  | .true, .false => rfl
  | .false, .true => rfl
  | .false, .false => rfl
/-- `Bool.or` is associative. -/
def Bool.or_assoc : (a b c : Bool) → Bool.or (Bool.or a b) c = Bool.or a (Bool.or b c)
  | .true, b, c => rfl
  | .false, b, c => rfl

/-- `.true` is a right identity of `Bool.and`. -/
def Bool.and_true : (a : Bool) → Bool.and a .true = a
  | .true => rfl
  | .false => rfl
/-- `.true` is a left identity of `Bool.and`. -/
def Bool.true_and (a : Bool) : Bool.and .true a = a := rfl
/-- `.false` is absorbing on the right for `Bool.and`. -/
def Bool.and_false : (a : Bool) → Bool.and a .false = .false
  | .true => rfl
  | .false => rfl
/-- `.false` is absorbing on the left for `Bool.and`. -/
def Bool.false_and (a : Bool) : Bool.and .false a = .false := rfl
/-- `.false` is a right identity of `Bool.or`. -/
def Bool.or_false : (a : Bool) → Bool.or a .false = a
  | .true => rfl
  | .false => rfl
/-- `.false` is a left identity of `Bool.or`. -/
def Bool.false_or (a : Bool) : Bool.or .false a = a := rfl
/-- `.true` is absorbing on the right for `Bool.or`. (`lib/Hdl/Temporal.wiz` uses the name
`Bool.or_true` for the elimination form of a true disjunction.) -/
def Bool.or_true_right : (a : Bool) → Bool.or a .true = .true
  | .true => rfl
  | .false => rfl
/-- `.true` is absorbing on the left for `Bool.or`. -/
def Bool.true_or (a : Bool) : Bool.or .true a = .true := rfl

/-- `Bool.and` is idempotent. -/
def Bool.and_self : (a : Bool) → Bool.and a a = a
  | .true => rfl
  | .false => rfl
/-- `Bool.or` is idempotent. -/
def Bool.or_self : (a : Bool) → Bool.or a a = a
  | .true => rfl
  | .false => rfl

/-- Absorption: `a && (a || b) = a`. -/
def Bool.and_absorb : (a b : Bool) → Bool.and a (Bool.or a b) = a
  | .true, b => rfl
  | .false, b => rfl
/-- Absorption: `a || (a && b) = a`. -/
def Bool.or_absorb : (a b : Bool) → Bool.or a (Bool.and a b) = a
  | .true, b => rfl
  | .false, b => rfl

/-- `Bool.and` distributes over `Bool.or`. -/
def Bool.and_or_distrib : (a b c : Bool) →
    Bool.and a (Bool.or b c) = Bool.or (Bool.and a b) (Bool.and a c)
  | .true, b, c => rfl
  | .false, b, c => rfl
/-- `Bool.or` distributes over `Bool.and`. -/
def Bool.or_and_distrib : (a b c : Bool) →
    Bool.or a (Bool.and b c) = Bool.and (Bool.or a b) (Bool.or a c)
  | .true, b, c => rfl
  | .false, b, c => rfl

/-- Negation is an involution. -/
def Bool.not_not : (a : Bool) → Bool.not (Bool.not a) = a
  | .true => rfl
  | .false => rfl
/-- `!true = false`. -/
def Bool.not_true : Bool.not .true = .false := rfl
/-- `!false = true`. -/
def Bool.not_false : Bool.not .false = .true := rfl
/-- `a && !a = false`. -/
def Bool.and_not_self : (a : Bool) → Bool.and a (Bool.not a) = .false
  | .true => rfl
  | .false => rfl
/-- `a || !a = true`. -/
def Bool.or_not_self : (a : Bool) → Bool.or a (Bool.not a) = .true
  | .true => rfl
  | .false => rfl
/-- De Morgan: `!(a && b) = !a || !b`. -/
def Bool.not_and : (a b : Bool) → Bool.not (Bool.and a b) = Bool.or (Bool.not a) (Bool.not b)
  | .true, .true => rfl
  | .true, .false => rfl
  | .false, .true => rfl
  | .false, .false => rfl
/-- De Morgan: `!(a || b) = !a && !b`. -/
def Bool.not_or : (a b : Bool) → Bool.not (Bool.or a b) = Bool.and (Bool.not a) (Bool.not b)
  | .true, .true => rfl
  | .true, .false => rfl
  | .false, .true => rfl
  | .false, .false => rfl

-- ## Booleans as propositions
--
-- `b = .true` is the proposition a Boolean stands for; these are its bridges to `∧`,
-- `∨` and `¬`, and the way a `Bool` case split used to feed a proposition.

/-- `a && b = true` exactly when both are true. -/
def Bool.and_eq_true : (a b : Bool) → ((Bool.and a b = .true) ↔ (a = .true ∧ b = .true))
  | .true, .true => Iff.intro (fun h => And.intro rfl rfl) (fun h => rfl)
  | .true, .false => Iff.intro (fun h => absurd h.1) (fun h => absurd h.2.1)
  | .false, .true => Iff.intro (fun h => absurd h.1) (fun h => absurd h.1.1)
  | .false, .false => Iff.intro (fun h => absurd h.1) (fun h => absurd h.1.1)
/-- `a && b = false` exactly when one of them is false. -/
def Bool.and_eq_false : (a b : Bool) → ((Bool.and a b = .false) ↔ (a = .false ∨ b = .false))
  | .true, .true => Iff.intro (fun h => absurd h.1) (fun h => Or.elim h (fun ha => absurd ha.1) (fun hb => absurd hb.1))
  | .true, .false => Iff.intro (fun h => Or.inr rfl) (fun h => rfl)
  | .false, .true => Iff.intro (fun h => Or.inl rfl) (fun h => rfl)
  | .false, .false => Iff.intro (fun h => Or.inl rfl) (fun h => rfl)
/-- `a || b = true` exactly when one of them is true. -/
def Bool.or_eq_true : (a b : Bool) → ((Bool.or a b = .true) ↔ (a = .true ∨ b = .true))
  | .true, .true => Iff.intro (fun h => Or.inl rfl) (fun h => rfl)
  | .true, .false => Iff.intro (fun h => Or.inl rfl) (fun h => rfl)
  | .false, .true => Iff.intro (fun h => Or.inr rfl) (fun h => rfl)
  | .false, .false => Iff.intro (fun h => absurd h.1) (fun h => Or.elim h (fun ha => absurd ha.1) (fun hb => absurd hb.1))
/-- `a || b = false` exactly when both are false. -/
def Bool.or_eq_false : (a b : Bool) → ((Bool.or a b = .false) ↔ (a = .false ∧ b = .false))
  | .true, .true => Iff.intro (fun h => absurd h.1) (fun h => absurd h.1.1)
  | .true, .false => Iff.intro (fun h => absurd h.1) (fun h => absurd h.1.1)
  | .false, .true => Iff.intro (fun h => absurd h.1) (fun h => absurd h.2.1)
  | .false, .false => Iff.intro (fun h => And.intro rfl rfl) (fun h => rfl)
/-- `!a = true` exactly when `a = false`. -/
def Bool.not_eq_true : (a : Bool) → ((Bool.not a = .true) ↔ (a = .false))
  | .true => Iff.intro (fun h => absurd h.1) (fun h => absurd h.1)
  | .false => Iff.intro (fun h => rfl) (fun h => rfl)
/-- `!a = false` exactly when `a = true`. -/
def Bool.not_eq_false : (a : Bool) → ((Bool.not a = .false) ↔ (a = .true))
  | .true => Iff.intro (fun h => rfl) (fun h => rfl)
  | .false => Iff.intro (fun h => absurd h.1) (fun h => absurd h.1)
/-- `!a = true` exactly when `a` is not true. -/
def Bool.not_true_iff : (a : Bool) → ((Bool.not a = .true) ↔ ¬ (a = .true))
  | .true => Iff.intro (fun h => absurd h.1) (fun h => absurd (h rfl))
  | .false => Iff.intro (fun h => fun ht => absurd ht.1) (fun h => rfl)
/-- The bridge `b = true ↔ ¬ (b = false)`. -/
def Bool.eq_true_iff : (b : Bool) → ((b = .true) ↔ ¬ (b = .false))
  | .true => Iff.intro (fun h => fun hf => Bool.trueNeFalse hf) (fun h => rfl)
  | .false => Iff.intro (fun h => absurd h.1) (fun h => absurd (h rfl))
/-- The bridge `b = false ↔ ¬ (b = true)`. -/
def Bool.eq_false_iff : (b : Bool) → ((b = .false) ↔ ¬ (b = .true))
  | .true => Iff.intro (fun h => absurd h.1) (fun h => absurd (h rfl))
  | .false => Iff.intro (fun h => fun ht => absurd ht.1) (fun h => rfl)

-- ## The default `simp` set
--
-- The Boolean algebra above, oriented the way `simp` rewrites
-- (`docs/stages/06-macros/simp.md`). The rules are stated with `Bool.and`/`Bool.or`/
-- `Bool.not`, which are the terms `a && b`, `a || b` and `!a` elaborate to (the `&&` and
-- `||` macros are a `match` on the first argument, the same stuck term as the function
-- applied to a variable), so a goal written with the notation simplifies too.
--
-- What is **not** registered, and why:
--
-- * `Bool.true_and`, `Bool.false_and`, `Bool.false_or`, `Bool.true_or`, `Bool.not_true`,
--   `Bool.not_false`: the left side computes to a variable or a data value. The goal has
--   already computed it, so the rule could never match; use the lemma explicitly when a
--   proof needs the equation as a term.
-- * `Bool.and_comm`, `Bool.or_comm`, `Bool.and_assoc`, `Bool.or_assoc`: commutativity and
--   associativity are not oriented. `a && b → b && a` would loop, and the associativity
--   rules have no normal form to aim at (the same reason `Nat.add_comm` stays out).
-- * `Bool.and_or_distrib`, `Bool.or_and_distrib`: they expand the term, and with both
--   registered `simp` could grow a goal without bound.
-- * `Bool.and_eq_true`, `Bool.and_eq_false`, `Bool.or_eq_true`, `Bool.or_eq_false`,
--   `Bool.not_eq_true`, `Bool.not_eq_false`, `Bool.not_true_iff`, `Bool.eq_true_iff`,
--   `Bool.eq_false_iff`: a rule must be an equation; these are `↔`, a pair of
--   implications, not `=`.
-- * `Bool.cases`, `Bool.trueNeFalse`, `Bool.and_left`, `Bool.and_right`: a disjunction, a
--   negation, and two lemmas with a premise before the equation (a rule with a premise
--   needs a discharger, S3).
-- * The equality section (`Eq.symm`, `Eq.trans`, `Eq.congrArg`, `Eq.substProp`,
--   `Iff.toEq`, `Iff.ofEq`, `propext`, `funext`, `congrFun`, `Eq.transProp`): every one
--   has a premise before the equation, so `attribute [simp]` rejects it until S3.

attribute [simp] Bool.not_not
attribute [simp] Bool.not_and
attribute [simp] Bool.not_or
attribute [simp] Bool.and_true
attribute [simp] Bool.and_false
attribute [simp] Bool.and_self
attribute [simp] Bool.and_absorb
attribute [simp] Bool.and_not_self
attribute [simp] Bool.or_false
attribute [simp] Bool.or_true_right
attribute [simp] Bool.or_self
attribute [simp] Bool.or_absorb
attribute [simp] Bool.or_not_self
